## TIẾNG ANH · 16:30–17:00 — Thứ Năm: 3 câu phỏng vấn

**Q1. "Have you worked with push notifications?"**
> *"Both sides, which is the part I'd emphasize. On the client it's permissions, tokens, and channels — that's the easy half. The interesting half is the server: batching into groups of a hundred, and understanding that a 200 response doesn't mean delivered. It means Expo accepted the job. You get a ticket, and fifteen minutes later you fetch a receipt, and the receipt is where the truth is."*

**Q2. "What happens to tokens when a user uninstalls?"**
> *"The receipt comes back DeviceNotRegistered, and you delete the row. If you don't, three things go wrong. Your table grows forever, you waste work on every send, and — the one people miss — Apple and Google deprioritize your whole app if your invalid token rate is high. So dead tokens actively hurt your real users. Cleanup isn't housekeeping, it's deliverability."*

**Q3. "When do you ask for notification permission?"**
> *"Never on first launch. On iOS a denial is permanent — you can't ask again, you can only send them to Settings. So I ask right after the user turns on meal reminders, because at that moment they've told me they want them. It costs nothing to wait and it roughly doubles the accept rate."*

**5 từ mới:**

| Từ / cụm | Nghĩa | Câu mẫu |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `deliverability` | khả năng gửi tới nơi | *Cleanup isn't housekeeping, it's deliverability.* |
| `deprioritize` (v) | giảm độ ưu tiên | *They deprioritize your whole app.* |
| `the truth is in X` | sự thật nằm ở X | *The receipt is where the truth is.* |
| `permanent denial` | từ chối vĩnh viễn | *On iOS a denial is permanent.* |
| `cold start` | khởi động lạnh | *Tapping it cold starts the app.* |

